body:
**Describe the problem**

Accessing a data member through a splice with `->` when the base is an lvalue of pointer type trips an assertion in `Sema::BuildMemberExpr`:

```cpp
#include <meta>

struct Vtable {
  int (*entry)(int);
};

int use(const Vtable* vtable) {
  constexpr std::meta::info member = ^^Vtable::entry;
  return vtable->[:member:](1);
}
```

```
clang/lib/Sema/SemaExprMember.cpp:844: MemberExpr *clang::Sema::BuildMemberExpr(...):
Assertion `(!IsArrow || Base->isPRValue()) && "-> base must be a pointer prvalue"' failed.
...
#9  clang::Sema::BuildMemberExpr(clang::Expr*, bool, clang::SourceLocation, clang::NestedNameSpecifierLoc, ...)
#10 clang::Sema::BuildFieldReferenceExpr(clang::Expr*, bool, clang::SourceLocation, clang::CXXScopeSpec const&, clang::FieldDecl*, ...)
#11 clang::Sema::BuildMemberReferenceExpr(clang::Expr*, clang::QualType, clang::SourceLocation, bool, clang::CXXScopeSpec const&, ...)
#12 clang::Sema::BuildMemberReferenceExpr(clang::Scope*, clang::Expr*, clang::SourceLocation, clang::tok::TokenKind, clang::CXXSpliceExpr*)
#13 clang::Parser::ParsePostfixExpressionSuffix(clang::ActionResult<clang::Expr*, true>)
```

The same assertion fires from template instantiation (`TreeTransform<TemplateInstantiator>::TransformCallExpr` → the same `BuildMemberReferenceExpr` overload) when the splice operand is a template parameter. `vtable->entry(1)` and `(*vtable).[:member:](1)` are fine.

Observed with clang-p2996 060be17654102019e14810c3f948ef85a490755f, `-std=c++26 -freflection-latest -stdlib=libc++`, assertions enabled. A release build accepts the program and generates correct code, so this is likely benign in practice, but it makes every asserting build (and anything built on it, such as a debug clang-tidy or clangd) unusable on code that uses `->[: :]` with an lvalue pointer. GCC trunk accepts the program.

**Cause**

`Sema::BuildMemberReferenceExpr(Scope*, Expr* Base, SourceLocation, tok::TokenKind, CXXSpliceExpr*)` (SemaExprMember.cpp) checks that `Base` is a pointer when `->` is used but never applies `DefaultFunctionArrayLvalueConversion` (or `PerformMemberExprBaseConversion`) to it, unlike `Sema::ActOnMemberAccessExpr`, so the `MemberExpr` is built with an lvalue base.

**Environment**

- clang-p2996 at 060be17654102019e14810c3f948ef85a490755f, `-DLLVM_ENABLE_ASSERTIONS=ON`, Linux x86_64.

**Describe the problem**

`^^derived::operator()` is rejected with `error: cannot take the reflection of a using-declarator` when the call operator is introduced into `derived` by a using-declaration:

```cpp
struct base {
  int operator()(int x) const { return x; }
};

struct derived : base {
  using base::operator();
};

constexpr auto r = ^^derived::operator();  // error: cannot take the reflection of a using-declarator
static_assert(r == ^^base::operator());    // GCC trunk: passes
```

Observed with clang-p2996 060be17654102019e14810c3f948ef85a490755f (`-std=c++26`, with either `-freflection` or `-freflection-latest`). GCC trunk (17.0.0 20260816, `-std=c++26 -freflection`) accepts both lines: the reflection compares equal to `^^base::operator()`.

**Why this case seems distinct from #230**

[expr.reflect]/5.1 — "if lookup finds a declaration that replaced a using-declarator during a single search, R is ill-formed" — belongs to the `^^reflection-name` form, and a *reflection-name* is limited to identifiers (`nested-name-specifier(opt) identifier`). `operator()` is an *operator-function-id*, so `^^derived::operator()` can only parse as `^^id-expression` and is governed by [expr.reflect]/7: the id-expression denotes an overload set, `&derived::operator()` is well-formed and selects a unique function, so per (7.2) R represents that function — and paragraph 7 has no using-declarator carve-out.

So unlike `^^std::int32_t` (#230) or `^^derived::fn` for an identifier `fn` — where /5.1 applies and the rejection is correct — this one looks like rejects-valid. Is the blanket rejection of `UsingShadowDecl` on the id-expression path intended?

GCC 🟢 Clang 🔴 https://godbolt.org/z/a3b1zz5M3
```cpp
constexpr struct {
    decltype(^^::) _; // remove -> clang accept
    static auto f(int&) {}
} a {};

int main() {
    int i;
    a.f(i);
}
```
a.f(i) is well-formed because f is a static member function and the object expression is not evaluated. Clang incorrectly propagates the consteval-only property of the object type to the entire member access expression, causing a valid program to be rejected. GCC accepts the code.
