## Summary

`Sema::PopExpressionEvaluationContext` binds a reference into `Sema::ExprEvalContexts` (a `SmallVector<ExpressionEvaluationContextRecord, 8>`):

```cpp
ExpressionEvaluationContextRecord& Rec = ExprEvalContexts.back();
...
HandleImmediateInvocations(*this, Rec);
// Rec used again below (VolatileAssignmentLHSs, ParentCleanup, SavedMaybeODRUseExprs, ...)
```

`HandleImmediateInvocations` constant-evaluates the popped context's pending immediate (consteval) invocations. With reflection, that evaluation **reenters Sema**: `substitute()` instantiates the named specialization via `Sema::InstantiateFunctionDeclaration` (and, for an undeduced signature, its body — `MetaActions::Substitute`), the evaluator's `EnsureInstantiated` hook instantiates definitions for calls, and every nested instantiation pushes/pops expression evaluation contexts. Once `ExprEvalContexts` grows past its current capacity it reallocates — and `Rec` (plus the post-loop uses inside `HandleImmediateInvocations`, plus the tail of `PopExpressionEvaluationContext`) dangles into freed memory.

This is reflection-specific reentrancy: ordinary constant evaluation never calls back into Sema, which is presumably why the reference-holding has been safe upstream. The function already half-knows about reentrancy — there is a `NOTE(P2996)` switching the candidate loop to index-based iteration because evaluation can grow the *candidate vector* — but the outer record reference has the same problem one level up.

We hit this in the field reflecting `nlohmann::json` (a reflection-driven binding generator): a non-deterministic SIGSEGV at a constant instruction in `Sema::PopExpressionEvaluationContext` with **ASCII-looking garbage fault addresses** varying across runs (`0x4c453734634c4538`, `0x634c453734634c4d`, `0x74` — the freed block had been reused for `define_static_string` text). It originally masqueraded as a `-fconstexpr-steps` problem: the default step budget stopped evaluation before the corruption could surface, so *raising* the budget looked like it "ICE'd the compiler".

## Reproducer

Self-contained, `-std=c++26 -freflection-latest`, validated at `837da39eb88c` (current `p2996` tip). The cascade must run **inside the deferred evaluation** — i.e. nothing may instantiate at parse time — so the depth is passed as an evaluation-time value and the recursion happens through instantiation:

- `start(n)` is consteval, called in a **runtime** function body → deferred as an immediate-invocation candidate, evaluated inside `HandleImmediateInvocations` while `Rec` is held;
- its evaluation calls `substitute(^^chain, {n})`; `chain<N>` has a **deduced return type**, so `MetaActions::Substitute` instantiates the body;
- the body recurses through a **dependent splice-of-substitute**, resolved per instantiation — 64 nested instantiations, each pushing evaluation contexts, crossing several SmallVector growth thresholds inside the held-reference window.

<details>
<summary>repro.cpp</summary>

```cpp
#include <meta>

#include <cstdio>
#include <vector>

template <int N>
consteval auto chain() {  // deduced return type => substitute() instantiates the body
  if constexpr (N <= 0) {
    return 0;
  } else {
    // Dependent splice: resolved during THIS specialization's instantiation,
    // evaluating substitute(^^chain, {N-1}) then — recursing the instantiation
    // one level deeper while every outer instantiation frame is still pushed.
    return [:std::meta::substitute(
        ^^chain,
        std::vector<std::meta::info>{std::meta::reflect_constant(N - 1)}):]() + 1;
  }
}

consteval int start(int n) {
  // `n` exists only at constant-evaluation time, so this substitute() — and the
  // whole chain<n> instantiation cascade behind it — runs while
  // HandleImmediateInvocations is evaluating THIS call.
  return std::meta::extract<int (*)()>(std::meta::substitute(
      ^^chain, std::vector<std::meta::info>{std::meta::reflect_constant(n)}))();
}

int main() {
  // Runtime context: start(64) is deferred as an immediate-invocation candidate
  // and evaluated at PopExpressionEvaluationContext with `Rec` held.
  int x = start(64);
  std::printf("chain depth evaluated: %d\n", x);
  return x == 64 ? 0 : 1;
}
```

</details>

## Expected

Compiles and runs; prints `chain depth evaluated: 64`.

## Actual (at `837da39eb88c`)

Use-after-free. Because the crash depends on reuse of the freed block, plain runs are non-deterministic (we measured 0 crashes in 10 `-fsyntax-only` runs on macOS — silent latent corruption). Two ways to make it deterministic:

- **Address probe**: instrumenting `PopExpressionEvaluationContext` to compare `&ExprEvalContexts.back()` before/after `HandleImmediateInvocations` fires on every compile of the repro (the record moved; `Rec` dangles).
- **Scribbled allocator**: `MallocScribble=1` (macOS) crashes **5/5** with SIGSEGV inside `clang::Sema::PopExpressionEvaluationContext` — the exact frame from the field crash. ASan should behave equivalently.

## Fix

PR follows. Three parts:

1. `HandleImmediateInvocations`: re-acquire the record via `currentEvaluationContext()` on each access in the (already index-based) candidate-evaluation loop, and re-acquire once after it for the trailing diagnostics loops — the record is always the top of the stack (nested contexts are balanced), only its address moves.
2. `PopExpressionEvaluationContext`: re-acquire `ExprEvalContexts.back()` after `HandleImmediateInvocations` for the tail, and once more after `CleanupVarDeclMarking()` (which can also instantiate).
3. Same family, found by auditing every reference held into `ExprEvalContexts`: `CheckLValueToRValueConversionOperand` bound `auto &CEO = ExprEvalContexts.back().ConstevalOnly` **before** `rebuildPotentialResultsAsNonOdrUsed()` and inserted through it after — the rebuild can mark declarations used and instantiate. Re-acquire after the rebuild.

A regression test is included (`libcxx/test/std/experimental/reflection/consteval-reentrant-instantiation.pass.cpp`): the 64-deep cascade above. On a normal build a regression is heap-layout-dependent, but sanitizer/scribble configurations catch it deterministically.

## Summary

`mangleReflection`'s `ReflectionKind::Template` case encodes only the reflected template's *name*. Function templates can be overloaded, so reflections of two same-named sibling templates are mangled byte-identically. A function template taking such a reflection as an NTTP then gets one mangled name for two distinct specializations; the AST is correct, but CodeGen folds the linkonce_odr definitions by mangled name and a single body silently serves both call sites. No diagnostic is emitted at any point.

This bites any reflection-driven dispatcher that walks `members_of(^^T)` with `template for` and forwards each member to a helper as an NTTP — the natural shape for binding generators. Where we hit it: `absl::flat_hash_map<K,V>` exposes `operator[]` as a member-template pair (an instantiable overload plus a SFINAE-false lifetimebound sibling); a nanobind-style binding generator bound the map's entire heterogeneous query surface *except* `operator[]`, which vanished without any diagnostic because the sibling's (no-op) dispatch body was the one that survived the fold.

## Reproducer

Self-contained, `-std=c++26 -freflection-latest` (no other flags needed). Validated at `837da39eb88c` (current `p2996` tip).

Core shape:

```cpp
