## Expected

Two distinct `l2_inner<B_l2, ...>` specializations; call 0 reports the first `operator[]` (substitutable, `is_operator_function == true`), call 1 reports the pack sibling (`can_substitute == false`). I.e. the "level 2" section's output matches the "level 0"/"level 1" sections.

## Actual

Both calls execute the SAME body (whichever the fold kept — observed: the sibling's):

```
== level 2 (substitute inside nested dependent instantiation -- failing shape) ==
  [call 0] member#1 can_sub=0
  [call 1] member#1 can_sub=0
```

`nm` on the object shows a single `__Z8l2_innerI4B_l2MtNS0_ixIEEEEvi` — the `Mt...` reflection mangling carries no information distinguishing the overloads. `-ast-dump` confirms two correct, distinct `FunctionDecl` specializations with the right per-body constants; the loss is at mangling/CodeGen.

Notes that may save a triager time — it masquerades well:

- It looks like "`substitute()`/qualifier predicates misreport two dependent levels deep": the observable is a consteval predicate apparently answering wrong, but only because the wrong instantiation's body ran.
- It disappears when the dispatcher takes any additional discriminating NTTP, when only one of the siblings is dispatched, or when a *specialization* reflection (a declaration, which mangles with its full signature) is passed down instead of the template reflection — so shallow/direct-call bisects all look fine.

## Fix

PR with a fix + regression test follows shortly: for `FunctionTemplateDecl` reflections, append a `$`-bracketed ODR hash (`ODRHash::AddTemplateParameterList` + `AddFunctionDecl(pattern, /*SkipBody=*/true)`) after the template name. ODR hashes are cross-TU-stable (modules rely on this), preserving legitimate linkonce_odr merging.

We first tried the more Itanium-spirited encoding — `mangleTemplateParamDecl` per head parameter + requires-clauses + the pattern's function type — but it asserts on real-world dependent pattern types: `mangleFunctionParam`'s `parmDepth < FunctionTypeDepth.getDepth()` fires for parameter-referencing expressions (Abseil's lifetimebound SFINAE / `noexcept(...)`) mangled outside a function-declaration context. If a structural encoding is preferred, that assertion path needs handling first.

