To be honest, after all those lambda things, I am not really sure whether it's just me getting the spec wrong.

But it seems to me that when reflecting a type alias, even though it represents an anonymous type, it should reflect the type alias itself?

We discussed it some time ago offline but decided to put it here to not forget later.

If we have code like this:
```cpp
#include <meta>

struct S {
  int val;

  S() = default;
  S(int i) : val(i) {}
  S(double i) : val(i) {}
};

int main() {
    consteval {
     [[maybe_unused]] auto r = ^^S::S;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

Clang fork allows to take reflection on constructors using `^^S::S` way. However, it's not clear which exact constructor overload user expects to reflect and store to variable `r`.

Current implementation in GCC rejects such reflection, which is correct from my perspective.
