Changing `template for` to `expand` solves the issue (the code compiles and works correctly):
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <meta>


template <typename T>
void print_fields()
{
    constexpr std::meta::info class_info = ^^T;

    std::cout << std::meta::identifier_of(class_info) << ":\n";

    [:
        std::meta::detail::pretty_printer<char>::expand
        (
            nonstatic_data_members_of(class_info, std::meta::access_context::unchecked())
        )
    :] >> [&]<auto field>()
    {
        constexpr std::string_view field_name = std::meta::identifier_of(field);

        constexpr auto field_info = std::meta::display_string_of(std::meta::type_of(field));

        std::string access_modifier;

        if constexpr (std::meta::is_public(field))
        {
            access_modifier = "public";
        }
        else if constexpr (std::meta::is_protected(field))
        {
            access_modifier = "protected";
        }
        else
        {
            access_modifier = "private";
        }

        std::cout << "  " << access_modifier << " " << field_name << ": " << field_info << " " << "\n";
    };
}

struct Car
{
    int cost;
    int amountOfWheels;

private:
    std::string owner;
};

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    print_fields<Car>();
}
``` 


**Expected behavior**
In all other functions I used `expand` statement from `pretty_printer` to iterate over members and it worked. Then I decided to replace it with `template for` (I didn't know in the beginning it was implemented). All functions works correctly except the one above. So I expect that 
```c++
[:
    std::meta::detail::pretty_printer<char>::expand
    (
        nonstatic_data_members_of(class_info, std::meta::access_context::unchecked())
    )
:] >> [&]<auto field>()
{
// Some code
};
```
And 
```c++
template for (constexpr auto field : std::define_static_array(std::meta::nonstatic_data_members_of(class_info, std::meta::access_context::unchecked())))
{
// Some code
}
```
Should work in the same way, but unfortunately they don't

**Environment:**
- Compiler: clang++ 21.0.0git (commit hash 4c3e6ae840c5edf4ed972a255e9059d1a92d879a)
- Flags: -stdlib=libc++ -freflection -fparameter-reflection -fexpansion-statements -freflection-latest
- OS: Ubuntu22.04

comments.body:
So there are multiple parts to this issue. 

- For the implementation of `std::int32_t` and other integer types with specified widths. The standard says it needs to match the ones defined in <stdint.h>, which comes from C. <cstdint> is a C-compatibility library - https://eel.is/c++draft/cstdint.syn 

- Also, in the standard, it says that in C++, standard libraries need to be defined within the std namespace. When working with C-compatibility libraries. the implementation detail is unspecified, meaning each compiler has to specify how it is and sticks to it. But the standard does provide one option, that is, declaring them in the global namespace and then inject into the std namespace via `using declaration`, so I guess that's why libc++, and libstdc++ do it. So this is not an implementation bug. - https://eel.is/c++draft/requirements#headers-5

- I agree that applying a reflection on std::int32_t should work. But unfortunately it is falling into the case of using-declaration under the hood. The reason why C++ doesn't have reflection on using-declaration yet is because the committee needs to decide what this means.

```cpp
struct A { void fn(); };
struct B : virtual A { using A::fn; };
struct C : virtual A { using A::fn; };
struct D : B, C {};
```

Now if you do `^^D::fn`, it is unclear what information we should provide, such as which base class this proxy entity is coming from.

By the way a simple workaround might be simply wrap whatever the type is as a template parameter so it is an explicit type instead of whatever kinds of usings or so.

https://godbolt.org/z/KrGcf3G15

```cpp
#include <meta>
#include <cstdint>

using namespace std::meta;

template <typename T>
consteval auto make_explicit_dealias()
{
    return std::meta::dealias(^^T);
}

static_assert(make_explicit_dealias<std::int32_t>() == dealias(^^::int32_t));
```

This would work no matter how `std::int32_t` is defined. Just that I am not really sure what its semantics would actually be.

