**Describe the bug**
```
Killed - processing time exceeded
Program terminated with signal: SIGKILL
```

**Expected behavior**
`static assertion failed 'auto operator()'` or so.

**Describe the bug**
When the input range is empty, `reflect_constant_array` returns reflection of a weird thing that doesn't meet the requirements in [[meta.define.static]/11](https://eel.is/c++draft/meta.define.static#11), [/12](https://eel.is/c++draft/meta.define.static#12).

It seems that the type of the reflected object is non-standard `const T[0]` but not `const std::array<T, 0>`, which behaves unexpectedly in type traits.

https://github.com/bloomberg/clang-p2996/blob/f72d85e5a0fd9c960b07b6f1c8875110701cb627/libcxx/include/meta#L2321-L2337

**To Reproduce**
Example:
```C++
#include <meta>

constexpr auto rat = type_of(std::meta::reflect_constant_array(std::initializer_list<int>{}));
static_assert(is_object_type(rat));
static_assert(!is_array_type(rat) && !is_class_type(rat) && !is_union_type(rat) && !is_scalar_type(rat), "???");

using T = [:rat:];
static_assert(sizeof(T) == 0, "???");
```

[Godbolt link](https://godbolt.org/z/PPjT7qaes)

**Expected behavior**
The example doesn't compile. An `const std::array<int, 0>` object is reflected.

**Describe the bug**
The following code fails to compile:
```
#include <array>
#include <iostream>

constexpr auto arr = std::array{1, 2, 3};

int main() {
    bool first = true;
    template for(constexpr auto a : arr)
    {
        auto f = [&first](int a) {
            if (first)
            {
                first = false;
                return a;
            }
            else
                return 2 * a;
        };
        std::cout << f(a) << std::endl;
    }
}
```

with error:
```
<source>:10:20: error: reference to local variable 'first' declared in enclosing function 'main'
