**Describe the bug**
Trying to splice in a type for an explicit destructor call fails.  I'm not 100% sure if this is intentional behavior or a bug but based on other splice expressions it seems like this should be something that can be done.  (I know I can work around this with `std::destroy_at`)

**To Reproduce**
```cpp
#include <experimental/meta>
struct test{};

int main()
{
    test value;
    value.~[:^^test:]();
}
```

Error output:
```
<source>:7:12: error: expected a class name after '~' to name a destructor
    7 |     value.~[:^^test:]();
      |            ^
```

**Expected behavior**
The program to compile without error and call the destructor.

comments.body:
Works as intended.

[[expr.prim.splice]/2.1.2](https://brevzin.github.io/cpp_proposals/2996_reflection/p2996r13.html#pnum_170)

> For a _splice-expression_ of the form _splice-specifier_, let _S_ be the construct designated by _splice-specifier_. The expression is ill-formed if _S_ is [...] a destructor

That said:

> [[class.dtor]/16](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.dtor#16) says:

> In an explicit destructor call, the destructor is specified by a `~` followed by a _type-name_ or _computed-type-specifier_ that denotes the destructor's class type.

Since _splice-type-specifier_ is a _computed-type-specifier_, this _should_ work (but does not (yet)):

```cpp
#include <meta>
struct test{};

int main()
{
    test value;
    value.~typename [:^^test:];
}
```

I'll look at it 👍

